# Protected Signature Experiences

The following are protected signature experiences and form the core identity of the Hathor website.

These are not ordinary animations.

They are part of the brand experience and must always preserve their choreography, pacing, timing, layering and emotional impact across desktop, tablet and phone.

## Protected Experience 1 — Homepage Hero

This includes:

* Gold Venetian strip animation
* Scroll-controlled strip progression
* Hero logo rise
* Letter stagger and landing
* Book Now button expansion
* Book Now letter-spacing animation
* Hero release timing

### Protection Rules

Do not:

* Convert to a one-time reveal
* Finish the strips too early
* Remove the scroll relationship
* Change the order of events
* Compress the animation into a short sequence
* Replace with a generic hero animation

You may:

* Optimise rendering
* Reduce GPU cost
* Shorten the scroll runway slightly for phones
* Use fewer strips where visually equivalent
* Replace expensive rendering with cheaper rendering

The visual story must remain identical.

---

## Protected Experience 2 — Four-Image Fog Storytelling Section

This includes:

* Four sequential image transitions
* Fog-rise transitions
* Large editorial text
* Image-over-text reveal
* Scroll-controlled storytelling
* Image stacking
* Per-image dwell time

### Protection Rules

Every image must complete its own sequence.

Never:

* Stop after the first image
* Collapse multiple stages into one
* Convert to a single reveal
* Remove the fog transition
* Remove the image-covering behaviour
* Reduce the section to static cards

The section must remain a cinematic four-stage story.

---

## Protected Experience 3 — Three-Layer Luxury Navigation

This includes:

* Dark gold panel
* Light gold panel
* Cream panel
* Sequential layered opening
* Sequential layered closing
* Link stagger
* Premium pacing

Never:

* Collapse into one drawer
* Open all layers simultaneously
* Remove stagger
* Replace with a standard mobile navigation
* Shorten into a generic fast transition

The menu should feel architectural rather than functional.

---

## Protected Experience 4 — Editorial Scroll Stories

This includes every section intentionally designed as a cinematic scroll experience.

Examples include:

* Slow image storytelling
* Editorial reveals
* Luxury product showcases
* Cruise storytelling
* Scroll-driven image transitions
* Long-form immersive sections

These sections are designed to control pacing.

Never automatically convert them into:

* One-time fades
* Simple IntersectionObserver reveals
* Generic scroll animations

Maintain:

* Reading rhythm
* Dwell time
* Scene progression
* Scroll relationship
* Luxury pacing

Optimise rendering without changing the storytelling.

---

## Protected Experience 5 — Luxury Page Transitions

This includes:

* Route transitions
* Page-entry choreography
* Gold reveals
* Curtain transitions
* Editorial transitions
* Cinematic loading sequences

Do not:

* Replace with instant page changes
* Replace with generic fades
* Shorten dramatically
* Remove the luxury feeling

Phone implementations may be lighter but must preserve the same experience.

---

# Protected Experience Rule

Whenever editing any protected experience:

1. Identify the current choreography.
2. Preserve the choreography.
3. Preserve the visual story.
4. Optimise the implementation.
5. Verify the animation still feels luxurious.
6. Compare against the previous working version before replacing logic.

If an optimisation changes the experience instead of the implementation:

STOP.

Explain the conflict and wait for approval before making that change.

Protected experiences must never be automatically redesigned during performance optimisation.
